# The considered-and-CLOSED records that must SURVIVE a later edit: an option
# REJECTED, or DEFERRED under a stated trigger. Read by
# `mth check --records .spec-records SPEC.md`.
#
# Format: `<id>` then whitespace then a marker, which runs to end of line and
# may contain spaces. `#` comments and blank lines are skipped. The marker is
# a SUBSTRING of the record, long enough to be specific and short enough that
# rewording the surrounding prose does not fire it: what must survive is the
# DECISION, not the sentence.
#
# WHY A SEPARATE FILE: every other structural rule asks "is this file well
# formed", which one text can answer. This one asks "is this decision still
# recorded", which is a claim about edits over time, and no single text can
# say what used to be in it. So the checker owns the rule and this repo owns
# the list.
#
# WHY IT IS NEARLY EMPTY TODAY, stated rather than left to look like an
# oversight: this repo keeps its rejected options in COMMIT MESSAGES -- every
# decision commit carries what was chosen, what was rejected, why, and what
# would reopen it. Only options recorded in the SPEC TEXT can be checked here,
# and so far one is. The open questions marked `?` are not records: they are
# decisions not yet made, and nothing is lost if they change.
#
# Adding a line here is how a newly closed option becomes load-bearing.
# Removing one is a reviewed decision: it says the option is no longer worth
# recording, which is exactly the trade this file forbids making silently.

# The binary name. Without this, a later reader re-proposes `ctr` -- it is the
# obvious short form, and nothing in the spec would say it was already tried
# and dropped because containerd ships that command on most Linux hosts.
V13  ctrm

# The PIN POLICY. Without this, a later reader proposes following a branch --
# it removes the bump commits, which look like pure overhead until you ask
# what changed. The answer is that the gate's rules would move with no diff
# to review, which is the one property a spec gate cannot give up.
V44  FOLLOWING a branch REJECTED
